<h2>Building an Agent</h2>
<p>An <strong>agent</strong> is a specialist worker assigned to a specific role. Creating one is as easy as creating a skill — all you need is the idea, and the AI writes the rest. Here we build a <strong>paper analysis agent</strong> and use it to analyze a downloaded PDF.</p>

<h3>1) Prepare the PDF</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Give it the URL of the paper's detail page</span>
https://arxiv.org/abs/....  Download the PDF file of this paper.</code></pre>
<p>Claude Code finds the PDF link on arXiv and downloads it into the current folder. (Reading the PDF is handled by the document-skills we installed earlier.)</p>

<h3>2) Create the agent</h3>
<p>To scope it to this project only, tell Claude Code to create it under <code>.claude/</code>.</p>
<pre><code>Add an agent that analyzes papers under .claude.</code></pre>
<p>The generated agent carries <strong>metadata</strong> like the following.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Name · description</strong> — what this agent does.</li>
    <li><strong>Tool permissions</strong> — reading files, running Bash commands, and so on.</li>
    <li><strong>Model selection</strong> — choose the model each agent uses (for example the best one, <code>opus</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Persona · principles</strong> — e.g. "an expert in academic paper analysis", "produce a report that conveys the core in five minutes".</li>
</ul>
<p>The analysis style (abstract-focused / limitation-focused / verification-heavy, and so on) can be <strong>refined further</strong> with follow-up requests.</p>

<h3>3) Check the list · run it</h3>
<p>An agent may not be loaded right after you create it. Use <code>/agents</code> to check which agents are available in the current folder, and if it does not show up, <code>/exit</code> and restart.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Attach the PDF as a reference and analyze it with the agent you made</span>
@paper.pdf  Analyze this paper using the paper-analyzer agent.</code></pre>

